Description: Leave the key focus where it is when Looking Glass is created.
 Looking Glass is created hidden, and adding the first page to its notebook selects it, which moves the key focus to
 that page or, when nothing in it can take the focus, to the notebook itself. The stage then has the key focus on an
 actor of the hidden Looking Glass, and the keys typed afterwards reach no window, until another window receives the
 focus. Main.createLookingGlass() creates Looking Glass the first time it is called, which extensions call too, Just
 Perfection a second after it is enabled, so the keys typed into the window focused right after the login were lost.
 .
 The notebook moves the key focus to a selected page only while it is shown. Looking Glass moves the key focus to its
 entry when it opens, as before.
Author: Mitar
Bug: https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/gnome-shell/-/work_items/9554
Forwarded: https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/gnome-shell/-/work_items/9554
Last-Update: 2026-11-25
--- a/js/ui/lookingGlass.js
+++ b/js/ui/lookingGlass.js
@@ -202,9 +202,13 @@
             return;
         }
 
-        // Focus the new tab before unmapping the old one
+        // Focus the new tab before unmapping the old one. A notebook which is
+        // not shown leaves the key focus where it is: Looking Glass adds its
+        // pages while it is created hidden, and the key focus would move from
+        // the focused window to the hidden notebook.
         const tabData = this._tabs[index];
-        if (!tabData.scrollView.navigate_focus(null, St.DirectionType.TAB_FORWARD, false))
+        if (this.mapped &&
+            !tabData.scrollView.navigate_focus(null, St.DirectionType.TAB_FORWARD, false))
             this.grab_key_focus();
 
         this._unselect();
